{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: escalation finally-restore targets a jobParams guess, not the captured ambient `GH_TOKEN`**

`escalateForkSilentUpdate` swaps env `GH_TOKEN` to `sourceToken` and restores to `silentTok || sourceTok`:

```javascript
} finally {
    if (swapped) {
        try { set_env_variable('GH_TOKEN', silentTok || sourceTok); } catch (eRes) {} // AC7: restored even on exception
    }
}
```

Every other swap site in this file restores to `sourceTok` — the documented tick ambient (`js/smAgent.js:562`, `:7132`, `:7238`, `:8026`), and `workflows/sm.yml:79` wires the engine env as `GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.SOURCE_GITHUB_TOKEN }}`. The code never captures the ambient value before the swap, so the restore target is an assumption: in any deployment where ambient `GH_TOKEN` ≠ `jobParams.silentToken` (e.g. the engine riding `SOURCE_GITHUB_TOKEN` while the reusable factory wires `silent-token: ${{ github.token }}`, per `.github/workflows/machine-sm.yml:88`), the FIRST escalated push flips the process-wide credential for the **rest of the tick** — all later `gh` calls (labels, comments, dispatches, merges, on other PRs too) ride `github-actions[bot]` instead of the tick PAT: actor attribution changes, and any PAT-only capability the remaining rules rely on goes missing mid-tick.

In the live fa deployment ambient == silentToken, so today's behavior is unaffected — please confirm the factory's ambient wiring; if it can ever differ, this becomes blocking.

Suggested fix (robust regardless of deployment):

```javascript
var ambient = null;
try { ambient = get_env_variable('GH_TOKEN'); } catch (eAmb) {}
...
} finally {
    if (swapped) {
        try { set_env_variable('GH_TOKEN', ambient || silentTok || sourceTok); } catch (eRes) {}
    }
}
```
